In PySpark/Databricks, the main DataFrame write modes are:

append
overwrite
error / errorifexists
ignore

1.append-Adds new records to the existing table without deleting old records.
append does not automatically check for duplicate business keys. If the same records arrive again, duplicates may be inserted

df.write.format('delta').mode('append').option('path',external_path).saveAsTable(catalog_silver.schema_silver.table_customer)

2. overwrite
Replaces the existing table data with the DataFrame data.

(
    df.write
    .format("delta")
    .mode("overwrite")
    .saveAsTable("silver_catalog.silver_schema.customer")
)

Use in real projects

Full-load pipelines
Rebuilding Gold summary tables
Small lookup/reference tables
Development and testing

3. error or errorifexists
Raises an error if the table or output already exists.
(
    df.write
    .format("delta")
    .mode("error")
    .saveAsTable("silver_catalog.silver_schema.customer")
)

This is also the default behavior when .mode() is not specified:

Use in real projects
Creating a table only once
Preventing accidental replacement
Initial table deployment
Catching duplicate execution errors

4. ignore
If the table already exists, Spark skips the write without raising an error.
(
    df.write
    .format("delta")
    .mode("ignore")
    .saveAsTable("silver_catalog.silver_schema.customer")
)

Use in real projects
Create-once initialization
Optional reference tables
Idempotent setup notebooks
Be careful: ignore may hide the fact that new incoming data was not written.

Partial overwrite using replaceWhere
Sometimes you should replace only one date or partition instead of the complete table.

(
    df.write
    .format("delta")
    .mode("overwrite")
    .option(
        "replaceWhere",
        "business_date = '2026-07-28'"
    )
    .saveAsTable("silver_catalog.silver_schema.transactions")
)

save() writes DataFrame data to a storage path.
saveAsTable() writes data and registers it as a table in Unity Catalog or the metastore.
